        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "01837a91-431b-5dbc-be29-2842b6f0d30d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Hibiscus waimeae, H. arnottianus, Hawaiian hibiscus, Urera kaalae\n- Places: Oʻahu, Molokaʻi, Hawaii\n- Habitats: coastal mesic forests, mixed mesic forests, wet forests\n- Associated species: Alyxia oliviformis, Antidesma platyphyllum, Asplenium kaulfusii, Athyrium spp., Canavalia spp.\n\nFacts:\n- Hawaiian hibiscus are seven species of hibiscus regarded as native to Hawaii.\n- H. arnottianus of Oʻahu and Molokaʻi and H. waimeae are the only Hawaiian hibiscuses that have white flowers.\n- H. waimeae inhabits coastal mesic, mixed mesic, and wet forests at elevations of 250 meters and higher.\n- Urera kaalae is associated with maile, hame, Asplenium kaulfusii, Athyrium species, and ʻāwikiwiki.\n- The associated plants of Urera kaalae include Alyxia oliviformis, Antidesma platyphyllum, Asplenium kaulfusii, Athyrium species, and Canavalia species.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
